From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 07:05:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t7001: Keep the dwi power state on

The dwi power state carries the SoC's voltage requests to the PMIC.
The boot chain leaves it on, but nothing on T7001 claims it (the T7000
handhelds' backlight does), so Linux switches it off as unused about
0.7 s into boot. From then on the GPU's voltage request never reaches
the PMIC and the first GPU register read hangs the bus.

Keep it on, as LLB and iPadOS do. With it on, the J81's GPU core ID
reads 0x00070500 on Linux, as on iPadOS (OrchardLinux/gpu a8x/
DWI-CORE-ID.md, measured by mattonik).

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t7001-pmgr.dtsi | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t7001-pmgr.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t7001-pmgr.dtsi
index 431ed5ff4..caa303f6f 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t7001-pmgr.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t7001-pmgr.dtsi
@@ -101,6 +101,12 @@ ps_dwi: power-controller@20120 {
 		#power-domain-cells = <0>;
 		#reset-cells = <0>;
 		label = "dwi";
+		/*
+		 * Carries the voltage requests to the PMIC: with it off, the GPU
+		 * rail never comes up. The boot chain leaves it on; nothing on
+		 * T7001 holds it, unlike the T7000 handhelds' backlight.
+		 */
+		apple,always-on;
 	};
 
 	ps_gpio: power-controller@20128 {
